/* book: lead: Signal conditioning turns a weak, noisy, non-linear sensor output into a clean standard voltage or current that the next stage can digitise safely. A strain gauge may give only 2 mV at full load; an ADC needs volts. Conditioning amplifies, filters, linearises and isolates. It also attenuates large signals and matches impedance so a high-resistance sensor is not loaded. You meet it in PLC input car */
/* book Figure 6.1.1: Top: a 2 mV bridge signal leaves the in-amp (gain 500, 54.0 dB) at 1.0 V, then passes a 10 kΩ, 1 µF low-pass, linearisation and an isolation barrier before the ADC. Below: the low-pass has fc = 15.9 Hz, so slow strain passes while 50 Hz hum is cut to 0.303 of its size. */
