/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A DAS is a chain.
   - The multiplexer is what makes a DAS cheap: one accurate converter serves many sensors.
   - Resolution is the smallest voltage change the converter can distinguish.
   - ΔV is the resolution in volts (one least significant bit, 1 LSB); VFS is the full-scale span in volts, the difference between the highest and lowest input, so a ±5 V range has VFS = 10 V; n is the ADC bit count and 2ⁿ the number of output c
   - Speed is shared.
   - fch is the samples per second each channel gets, in samples/s (S/s); ftotal is the total converter rate in S/s; N is the number of channels actually scanned; Tconv is the time allowed for one conversion, in seconds, including multiplexer se
   - The sampling rule follows from fch.
   - A sample-and-hold circuit closes a switch, charges a capacitor to the input voltage, then opens.
   - Three system architectures carry these blocks into the field.
*/
