/* book Worked example 2: Q: A bedside patient monitor shares one 16-bit ADC with a ±2.5 V input range among five channels: three ECG leads at 500 S/s each, one pulse-oximeter waveform at 100 S/s and one arterial-pressure channel at 250 S/s. The ECG front end has a gain of 500. Find (a) the total conversion rate, (b) the time per conversion, (c) the ADC resolution and its value referred to the ECG electrodes, and (d) the highest ECG frequency each lead can carry.
   1. (a) Total rate: 3 × 500 + 100 + 250 = 1850 S/s.
   2. (b) Time per conversion: 1 / 1850 = 5.405 × 10⁻⁴ s = 540.5 µs, which must cover multiplexer settling, acquisition and conversion.
   3. (c) Span = 2.5 − (−2.5) = 5 V; ΔV = 5 / 2¹⁶ = 5 / 65 536 = 76.3 µV. Referred to the electrodes: 76.3 µV / 500 = 0.153 µV.
   4. (d) Each ECG lead gets 500 S/s, so fmax < 500 / 2 = 250 Hz.
   5. Check: a diagnostic ECG needs about 0.05 to 150 Hz and a 1 mV R wave spans 1 mV / 0.153 µV ≈ 6500 codes, so both the bandwidth and the resolution are ample.
   Answer: The ADC runs at 1850 S/s, allowing 540.5 µs per conversion; one step is 76.3 µV at the ADC, 0.153 µV at the electrodes; each ECG lead carries up to 250 Hz. */
